/**
 * WM Pro — Cloudflare Workers configuration (Phase 5 K-Bkt 4).
 *
 * Runtime: Cloudflare Workers via @opennextjs/cloudflare.
 * Adapter output: .open-next/ (Worker + assets).
 *
 * Canon anchors:
 *  - Hosting Runbook §4 (Next.js → Cloudflare Workers baseline)
 *  - Migration Contract Phase 1 (Cloudflare must become code-owned)
 *  - Migration Contract Phase 5 (Cloudflare env vs runtime env)
 *
 * IMPORTANT — SECRETS:
 *  This file MUST NEVER contain secret values. Install runtime
 *  secrets via `npx wrangler secret put NAME` (terminal-only prompt,
 *  never appears on screen) or the Cloudflare dashboard's encrypted
 *  variables section. Non-secret public configuration lives in the
 *  `vars` block below (currently empty; add build-safe public values
 *  here if any accumulate).
 *
 * OpenNext will write `.open-next/worker.js` + `.open-next/assets/` on
 * `opennextjs-cloudflare build`. Wrangler consumes those artifacts.
 */
{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "wealthymindsets-pro",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-05-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },

  /**
   * SECRET NAMES — never values. See the SECRETS note above.
   *
   * This list is NOT hand-maintained. It is a MIRROR of
   * `workerRequiredSecretNames()` in src/lib/broker/providerReadiness.ts,
   * which derives it from boot-gating PLATFORM_SECRETS. Provider credentials
   * remain owned by provider readiness: an absent provider must report
   * NOT CONFIGURED without preventing the rest of the Worker from deploying.
   * A Sentinel (providerReadiness.wrangler.test.ts) fails
   * if the two ever disagree, so editing this array by hand without editing
   * the registry turns CI red by name. Edit the registry.
   *
   * Declaring this block also stops wrangler INFERRING the secret set from
   * .dev.vars / .env / process.env — the mechanism that lets a deployed
   * Worker silently lack a credential the developer's laptop happens to have.
   *
   * Why credentials are absent: wrangler's check is a flat
   * name-presence test and cannot express "either this name or that one."
   * Provider credentials, accepted aliases (including SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY),
   * and all-or-nothing alternative pairs are therefore owned by capability
   * readiness, which CAN express the real condition. Listing them here would
   * fail a deployment that can safely serve an honest degraded state.
   */
  "secrets": {
    "required": [
      "JWT_SECRET"
    ]
  }
}
